---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../styles/calculus.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';
---
<Base wide title="Calculus in motion" description="Learn derivatives by moving, stretching and perturbing mathematical objects. Nine interactive experiments connect slopes, logs, partial derivatives, gradients, curvature and accumulation." pageClass="calculus-page">
 <div class="cl-shell">
  <header class="cl-header"><p class="cl-eyebrow"><a href="/teaching">Teaching</a> / Interactive mathematics</p><div><h1>Calculus in motion.</h1><p>Change something. <br/>See what follows.</p></div><p class="cl-header-note">Nine experiments. Start with a small move; end with several variables. Every picture, number and equation follows the same inputs.</p></header>
  <section data-calculus aria-label="Interactive calculus experiments"><p>Loading the experiments…</p><noscript><p>These experiments need JavaScript. They cover derivatives as local change, the product and chain rules, logarithms, partial derivatives, gradients, Taylor approximations, optimization, and the fundamental theorem of calculus. Read the freely accessible references below for the underlying mathematics.</p></noscript></section>
  <footer class="cl-references"><div><h2>Explore further.</h2><p>The invitation to treat mathematics as an environment to explore is inspired by <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B1J6Ou4q8vE">Alan Becker’s <em>Animation vs. Math</em></a>. These are original interactive lessons and diagrams.</p><p><a href="/macroeconomics/">Put derivatives to work in the macroeconomics suite ↗</a></p></div><div><h3>Definitions &amp; further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/3-1-defining-the-derivative">OpenStax: defining the derivative</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/3-9-derivatives-of-exponential-and-logarithmic-functions">Exponential and logarithmic derivatives</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-5-the-chain-rule">Multivariable chain rule</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-6-directional-derivatives-and-the-gradient">Directional derivatives and gradients</a></li></ul><p>Functions and parameters are teaching examples. Colored maps and curves are sampled displays of analytic functions; they are not numerical proofs.</p></div></footer>
 </div>
</Base>
<script>import '../scripts/calculus-lab.mjs';</script>
